Quick test

```bash
pytest test_server.py -v
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector python server.py
```

---

Tools

Tool Purpose Cost risk
brute_genius_plan Validate space + objective, count combinations, compute cost estimate, return plan_id. Refuses if estimate > max_cost_usd. Does not execute anything. None
brute_genius_launch Provision workers and score every combination. Re-checks balance before dispatch. Yes — provisions and bills
brute_genius_status Live progress: shards done, combinations evaluated, best-so-far. None
brute_genius_results Top-k highest-scoring combinations, sorted descending. None
brute_genius_kill Stop every worker and shard for a plan. Preserves partial results. Always available. None
xpu_probe Report detected accelerators, provider rates, and current balance. None

Typical flow

1. brute_genius_plan with your space, objective, and a ceiling you are willing to spend.
2. Inspect the returned estimated_cost_usd and estimated_minutes. If either is unacceptable, do not proceed.
3. brute_genius_launch with the plan_id.
4. brute_genius_status while running.
5. brute_genius_results when done.
6. brute_genius_kill if anything goes wrong.

---

Resources

URI Contents
brutegenius://plans All plans and their current states
brutegenius://plans/{plan_id}/combinations First 1,000 combinations of a plan (paged; the full set is intentionally not materialized)
brutegenius://hardware Live accelerator inventory
brutegenius://pricing Per-chip USD/hour rates

---

Prompts

· define_search — walks the user through specifying axes, objective, cost ceiling, and hardware.
· interpret_results — asks the model to identify the best combination, find patterns in the top-k, and suggest a narrowed follow-up space.

---

Safety guarantees (current)

These are enforced by the code as it exists today. Each is exercised by a test in test_server.py.

1. Plan refuses over ceiling. brute_genius_plan raises rather than returning a plan_id when the estimate exceeds max_cost_usd. No plan record is created.
2. Hard caps. 64 nodes and 10⁹ combinations, both checked before any work is dispatched.
3. Registry-only objectives. Objectives are looked up by name. No caller string is passed to eval, exec, compile, pickle.loads, or import. This is structural, not policy.
4. Kill always works. brute_genius_kill is unconditional and stops every shard associated with a plan_id.
5. Bounded abort latency. xpu.batch_score checks the kill flag before each combination and again before each batch evaluation. Worst-case post-cancellation work is one combination, not one batch.
6. Launch re-checks balance. If balance <= 0 or balance < estimate, launch raises before provisioning any node.

Not yet guaranteed (roadmap — see next section)

· Launch does not currently recompute the cost estimate when chip or nodes are overridden at launch time. The ceiling check uses the plan-time estimate.
· MockProvider.balance is never debited. In the mock, launch cannot actually run out of money. Real providers will inherit the same shape until charge() is wired in.
· Partial provisioning failures are not rolled back — nodes created before the failure remain running until kill() is called.

---

Roadmap

Next

· Index-arithmetic sharding in enumerator.shard. Current implementation re-walks the full space per shard (Θ(N·T) work); the planned rewrite yields Θ(T) work using the already-shipped combo_at decoder.
· Recompute cost on launch overrides. Ceiling enforcement must reflect the effective chip and node count, not the plan-time values.
· Ledger charge before scoring. Add GPUProvider.charge(amount_usd); debit the estimate before dispatch; raise on insufficient funds.
· Transactional provisioning. Roll back partially provisioned nodes on failure.
· Progressive result population. Populate plan["results"] as shards complete, so kill() reports real partials.

Later

· True parallelism. Dispatch shards concurrently (asyncio.gather over executor threads or processes). Add a concurrency parameter, default 1, capped at MAX_NODES.
· Vectorized objectives. Torch-backed versions of sphere / rastrigin / rosenbrock operating on a batch tensor. device then actually influences execution.
· Remote execution. Implement RunPodProvider.run_command via SSH or a baked scoring image. Add GPUProvider.run_shard(shard_spec) so shards (space + start index + stride) ship to nodes without materializing combinations.
· Resume from checkpoint. Per-shard k_last persistence; resume from k_last + N with no coordination.
· Plan TTL / eviction. Bound _plans growth on long-running servers.
· Refund unused estimate on early kill (requires per-second billing surface).
· Structured logging. JSON lines with plan_id, shard_idx, event.

---

Architecture

```
server.py          FastMCP tools, resources, prompts; plan store; safety caps
enumerator.py      count(), cartesian(), shard(), combo_at()  — lazy, no materialization
objectives.py      closed registry of named scoring functions
xpu.py             accelerator detection; batch scoring loop with abort checks
provider.py        abstract GPUProvider interface
mock_provider.py   in-memory fleet for tests and local dev
runpod_provider.py RunPod SDK-backed provider (run_command stubbed)
```

Design rules:

· The enumerator is lazy. cartesian() yields; nothing materializes T combinations.
· Objectives are looked up by name from a closed registry. No caller-supplied code path exists.
· All spend-related operations go through GPUProvider so ceilings and balance checks live in one place.
· No stdout writes in stdio transport. Use ctx.info or _log (stderr).

---

Testing

```bash
pytest test_server.py -v
```

Tests use fastmcp.Client against the live in-memory server. Every test starts with a clean _plans store and a healthy mock balance. Coverage includes:

· Plan validation, ceiling refusal, combination cap
· Unknown objective rejection
· Correct shard dispatch and full evaluation
· Sorted results and best-combination correctness (sphere, linear_sum)
· Kill semantics, including mid-run abort via monkeypatched slow scorer
· Status before launch
· Node-count cap
· Resource reads
· Accelerator probe

---

Patent notice

A U.S. utility patent application covering index-arithmetic sharding, registry-bound objective evaluation, two-tier abort checks, and pre-execution cost enforcement is in preparation. Inventor: Lewis James Seals Jr. This notice is not a claim of issued rights; it is disclosure of pending filings for defensive purposes.

