## What changed from the original, and why

| Original claim | Original text | Honest version |
|---|---|---|
| "shards it across a fleet of accelerators" | implied parallel remote execution | "shards it across workers"; parallel remote execution is a roadmap item |
| "Same scoring code runs on CUDA, XPU, MPS, or CPU" (in `xpu.py`) | implied device dispatch | device is detected and recorded; scoring runs on the host CPU until vectorized objectives land |
| "re-check balance before dispatch" | implied ledger | re-checks balance, does not debit it (mock cannot be drained); charge() is roadmap |
| "plan() refuses any estimate above max_cost_usd" | true at plan time, not at launch override | true at plan time; override gap is now explicitly listed under "Not yet guaranteed" |
| "kill() always stops every node" | true | unchanged, still true |
| "Objectives are registry-only; caller code is never executed" | true | unchanged, still true |

The `Status` section is the most important addition: it forces anyone reading the README to see what is built versus what is planned before they wire this into anything that touches a real account.

If you want to gate the README on the hardening pass instead — i.e., only publish once A1–A6 have landed — replace the entire **Status** section with:

```markdown
## Status

All capabilities described below are implemented and covered by tests. See `CHANGELOG.md` for the hardening pass that closed the gap between the original draft and the current release.
```

Only do that after the hardening commit lands and the tests are green. Publishing the gated version early would be the exact oversell this README was written to fix.
